WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.046 --> 00:11.646 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference November 2,

00:11.846 --> 00:19.546 align:middle
2022 CHAIR POWELL.

00:20.226 --> 00:23.056 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:23.056 --> 00:31.916 align:middle
My colleagues and I
are strongly committed

00:41.976 --> 00:56.556 align:middle
to bringing inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal.

00:57.406 --> 01:07.046 align:middle
We have both the
tools that we need

01:11.646 --> 01:48.866 align:middle
and the resolve it will take
to restore price stability

01:50.176 --> 02:14.406 align:middle
on behalf of American
families and businesses.

02:14.916 --> 02:33.086 align:middle
Price stability is
the responsibility

02:33.086 --> 04:27.176 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve
and serves

04:27.586 --> 04:32.706 align:middle
as the bedrock of our economy.

04:32.706 --> 04:34.436 align:middle
Without price stability,

04:34.436 --> 04:39.796 align:middle
the economy does
not work for anyone.

04:39.796 --> 04:42.486 align:middle
In particular, without
price stability,

04:42.486 --> 04:47.886 align:middle
we will not achieve
a sustained period

04:47.886 --> 04:55.556 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions that benefit all.

04:56.246 --> 05:01.386 align:middle
Today, the FOMC raised
our policy interest rate

05:01.386 --> 05:03.326 align:middle
by 75 basis points.

05:03.906 --> 05:07.996 align:middle
And we continue to anticipate

05:07.996 --> 05:11.496 align:middle
that ongoing increases
will be appropriate.

05:11.496 --> 05:17.546 align:middle
We are moving our policy
stance purposefully to a level

05:17.546 --> 05:19.286 align:middle
that will be sufficiently
restrictive

05:19.386 --> 05:21.086 align:middle
to return inflation
to 2 percent.

05:22.186 --> 05:23.936 align:middle
In addition, we are
continuing the process

05:23.936 --> 05:26.426 align:middle
of significantly reducing the
size of our balance sheet.

05:28.016 --> 05:30.786 align:middle
Restoring price stability will
likely require maintaining a

05:30.786 --> 05:33.296 align:middle
restrictive stance of
policy for some time.

05:34.846 --> 05:37.876 align:middle
I will have more to say about
today's monetary policy actions

05:37.876 --> 05:41.396 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

05:41.746 --> 05:45.266 align:middle
The U.S. economy has
slowed significantly

05:45.266 --> 05:47.496 align:middle
from last year's rapid pace.

05:48.686 --> 05:51.996 align:middle
Real GDP rose at a pace of
2.6 percent last quarter

05:52.176 --> 05:54.506 align:middle
but is unchanged
so far this year.

05:55.706 --> 05:58.306 align:middle
Recent indicators point to
modest growth of spending

05:58.306 --> 05:59.676 align:middle
and production this quarter.

06:01.396 --> 06:03.176 align:middle
Growth in consumer
spending has slowed

06:03.176 --> 06:04.476 align:middle
from last year's rapid pace,

06:04.476 --> 06:07.166 align:middle
in part reflecting lower
real disposable income

06:07.166 --> 06:08.626 align:middle
and tighter financial
conditions.

06:09.896 --> 06:12.436 align:middle
Activity in the housing sector
has weakened significantly,

06:12.666 --> 06:14.806 align:middle
largely reflecting
higher mortgage rates.

06:16.116 --> 06:18.916 align:middle
Higher interest rates and
slower output growth also appear

06:18.916 --> 06:20.956 align:middle
to be weighing on
business fixed investment.

06:23.306 --> 06:24.526 align:middle
Despite the slowdown in growth,

06:24.576 --> 06:26.556 align:middle
the labor market
remains extremely tight,

06:26.676 --> 06:28.746 align:middle
with the unemployment
rate at a 50-year low,

06:29.196 --> 06:33.256 align:middle
job vacancies still very high,
and wage growth elevated.

06:34.346 --> 06:37.466 align:middle
Job gains have been robust, with
employment rising by an average

06:37.466 --> 06:41.326 align:middle
of 289,000 jobs per month
over August and September.

06:43.096 --> 06:45.506 align:middle
Although job vacancies have
moved below their highs

06:45.836 --> 06:48.796 align:middle
and the pace of job gains has
slowed from earlier in the year,

06:49.326 --> 06:51.716 align:middle
the labor market continues
to be out of balance,

06:52.416 --> 06:54.906 align:middle
with demand substantially
exceeding the supply

06:54.906 --> 06:56.136 align:middle
of available workers.

06:58.076 --> 07:00.466 align:middle
The labor force participation
rate is little changed

07:00.466 --> 07:01.566 align:middle
since the beginning of the year.

07:03.506 --> 07:07.166 align:middle
Inflation remains well above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

07:07.816 --> 07:09.566 align:middle
Over the 12 months
ending in September,

07:09.566 --> 07:12.356 align:middle
total PCE prices
rose 6.2 percent;

07:12.826 --> 07:15.256 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

07:16.316 --> 07:18.706 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
5.1 percent.

07:19.726 --> 07:22.396 align:middle
And the recent inflation
data again have come

07:22.396 --> 07:23.656 align:middle
in higher than expected.

07:24.476 --> 07:26.936 align:middle
Price pressures remain
evident across a broad range

07:26.936 --> 07:27.916 align:middle
of goods and services.

07:28.526 --> 07:31.486 align:middle
Russia's war against Ukraine
has boosted prices for energy

07:31.486 --> 07:34.506 align:middle
and food and has created
additional upward pressure

07:34.506 --> 07:35.146 align:middle
on inflation.

07:37.156 --> 07:38.566 align:middle
Despite elevated inflation,

07:38.806 --> 07:40.966 align:middle
longer-term inflation
expectations appear

07:40.966 --> 07:44.186 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

07:44.186 --> 07:47.396 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters,

07:48.096 --> 07:50.006 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

07:50.736 --> 07:52.596 align:middle
But that is not grounds
for complacency;

07:53.316 --> 07:55.926 align:middle
the longer the current bout
of high inflation continues,

07:56.096 --> 07:58.016 align:middle
the greater the chance
that expectations

07:58.016 --> 07:59.956 align:middle
of higher inflation
will become entrenched.

08:01.336 --> 08:04.556 align:middle
The Fed's monetary
policy actions are guided

08:04.556 --> 08:06.806 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

08:06.896 --> 08:08.756 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

08:10.106 --> 08:11.896 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

08:12.136 --> 08:14.926 align:middle
that high inflation
imposes significant hardship

08:15.476 --> 08:19.196 align:middle
as it erodes purchasing power,
especially for those least able

08:19.196 --> 08:22.216 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

08:22.366 --> 08:23.696 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

08:25.066 --> 08:28.396 align:middle
We are highly attentive to the
risks that high inflation poses

08:28.396 --> 08:31.586 align:middle
to both sides of our mandate,
and we are strongly committed

08:31.586 --> 08:34.276 align:middle
to returning inflation to
our 2 percent objective.

08:35.716 --> 08:37.106 align:middle
At today's meeting,

08:37.106 --> 08:38.496 align:middle
the Committee raised
the target range

08:38.496 --> 08:41.136 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate by 75 basis points.

08:42.076 --> 08:43.616 align:middle
And we are continuing
the process

08:43.616 --> 08:46.426 align:middle
of significantly reducing the
size of our balance sheet,

08:46.956 --> 08:48.486 align:middle
which plays an important role

08:48.486 --> 08:50.386 align:middle
in firming the stance
of monetary policy.

08:52.376 --> 08:54.326 align:middle
With today's action, we
have raised interest rates

08:54.476 --> 08:56.966 align:middle
by 3  percentage
points this year.

08:57.826 --> 09:00.416 align:middle
We anticipate that ongoing
increases in the target range

09:00.416 --> 09:03.136 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
will be appropriate in order

09:03.136 --> 09:05.026 align:middle
to attain a stance
of monetary policy

09:05.026 --> 09:07.706 align:middle
that is sufficiently
restrictive to return inflation

09:07.706 --> 09:09.036 align:middle
to 2 percent over time.

09:10.606 --> 09:12.696 align:middle
Financial conditions have
tightened significantly

09:12.696 --> 09:14.296 align:middle
in response to our
policy actions,

09:14.536 --> 09:16.326 align:middle
and we are seeing
the effects on demand

09:16.636 --> 09:18.526 align:middle
in the most
interest-rate-sensitive sectors

09:18.526 --> 09:19.956 align:middle
of the economy, such as housing.

09:21.106 --> 09:23.146 align:middle
It will take time, however,
for the full effects

09:23.146 --> 09:24.866 align:middle
of monetary restraint
to be realized,

09:25.466 --> 09:26.806 align:middle
especially on inflation.

09:27.776 --> 09:29.106 align:middle
That's why we say
in our statement

09:29.106 --> 09:31.406 align:middle
that in determining the
pace of future increases

09:31.406 --> 09:33.306 align:middle
in the target range,
we will take

09:33.306 --> 09:35.946 align:middle
into account the cumulative
tightening of monetary policy

09:35.946 --> 09:39.596 align:middle
and the lags with which monetary
policy affects economic activity

09:39.596 --> 09:40.166 align:middle
and inflation.

09:42.006 --> 09:44.646 align:middle
At some point-as I've said

09:44.646 --> 09:47.356 align:middle
in the last two press
conferences-it will become

09:47.396 --> 09:49.296 align:middle
appropriate to slow
the pace of increases

09:49.496 --> 09:51.256 align:middle
as we approach the
level of interest rates

09:51.256 --> 09:53.306 align:middle
that will be sufficiently
restrictive to bring inflation

09:53.306 --> 09:54.406 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal.

09:55.586 --> 09:57.176 align:middle
There is significant uncertainty

09:57.176 --> 09:59.116 align:middle
around that level
of interest rates.

09:59.866 --> 10:04.296 align:middle
Even so, we still have some
ways to go, and incoming data

10:04.296 --> 10:07.536 align:middle
since our last meeting
suggest that the ultimate level

10:07.536 --> 10:10.446 align:middle
of interest rates will be
higher than previously expected.

10:11.076 --> 10:14.736 align:middle
Our decisions will depend on
the totality of incoming data

10:14.736 --> 10:16.146 align:middle
and their implications
for the outlook

10:16.146 --> 10:17.796 align:middle
for economic activity
and inflation.

10:18.656 --> 10:20.806 align:middle
We will continue to make our
decisions meeting by meeting

10:20.806 --> 10:22.996 align:middle
and communicate our thinking
as clearly as possible.

10:22.996 --> 10:27.986 align:middle
We are taking forceful steps to
moderate demand so that it comes

10:27.986 --> 10:29.456 align:middle
into better alignment
with supply.

10:30.126 --> 10:32.586 align:middle
Our overarching focus
is using our tools

10:32.586 --> 10:35.456 align:middle
to bring inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal

10:35.906 --> 10:38.026 align:middle
and to keep longer-term
inflation expectations

10:38.026 --> 10:38.676 align:middle
well anchored.

10:40.326 --> 10:44.066 align:middle
Reducing inflation is likely
to require a sustained period

10:44.176 --> 10:46.756 align:middle
of below-trend growth
and some softening

10:46.756 --> 10:47.916 align:middle
of labor market conditions.

10:49.056 --> 10:52.036 align:middle
Restoring price stability is
essential to set the stage

10:52.036 --> 10:53.496 align:middle
for achieving maximum employment

10:53.496 --> 10:55.176 align:middle
and stable prices
in the longer run.

10:56.476 --> 10:58.606 align:middle
The historical record
cautions strongly

10:58.606 --> 11:00.476 align:middle
against prematurely
loosening policy.

11:00.986 --> 11:03.396 align:middle
We will stay the course
until the job is done.

11:04.926 --> 11:07.856 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

11:07.856 --> 11:09.576 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

11:10.486 --> 11:12.896 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

11:14.206 --> 11:15.796 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

11:15.796 --> 11:17.366 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

11:17.366 --> 11:18.706 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

11:19.666 --> 11:21.476 align:middle
Thank you, and I look
forward to your questions.

11:22.576 --> 11:23.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:25.706 --> 11:26.756 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

11:26.986 --> 11:27.486 align:middle
Thank you.

11:27.486 --> 11:29.066 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

11:29.126 --> 11:32.056 align:middle
On the need to slow the pace of
rate increases at some point:

11:32.466 --> 11:34.836 align:middle
Is a downshift contingent
on a string

11:34.836 --> 11:38.576 align:middle
of better inflation data
specifically between now and,

11:39.036 --> 11:40.196 align:middle
let's say, the December meeting?

11:40.226 --> 11:43.386 align:middle
Or is that something that the
Fed could potentially proceed

11:43.386 --> 11:45.366 align:middle
with, independent of that data,

11:45.366 --> 11:46.926 align:middle
given the lagged effects
that you mentioned?

11:47.116 --> 11:47.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:48.266 --> 11:50.436 align:middle
So a couple things on that.

11:51.806 --> 11:54.336 align:middle
We do need to see inflation
coming down decisively,

11:54.336 --> 11:57.026 align:middle
and good evidence of
that would be a series

11:57.026 --> 11:58.296 align:middle
of down monthly readings.

11:58.686 --> 12:00.316 align:middle
Of course, that's what
we'd all love to see,

12:00.436 --> 12:03.566 align:middle
but that's-I've never thought
of that as the appropriate test

12:03.566 --> 12:05.096 align:middle
for slowing the pace
of increases

12:05.466 --> 12:07.766 align:middle
or for identifying the
appropriately restrictive level

12:07.766 --> 12:09.736 align:middle
that we're aiming for.

12:10.036 --> 12:11.496 align:middle
We need to bring
our policy stance

12:11.496 --> 12:13.646 align:middle
down to a level that's
sufficiently restrictive

12:13.646 --> 12:15.536 align:middle
to bring inflation down
to our 2 percent objective

12:16.056 --> 12:16.976 align:middle
over the medium term.

12:17.776 --> 12:19.506 align:middle
How will we know that
we've reached that level?

12:19.696 --> 12:22.436 align:middle
Well, we'll take into account
the full range of analysis

12:22.436 --> 12:23.846 align:middle
and data that bear
on that question,

12:24.416 --> 12:25.566 align:middle
guided by our assessment

12:25.566 --> 12:28.326 align:middle
of how much financial
conditions have tightened,

12:28.416 --> 12:30.336 align:middle
the effects that that
tightening is actually having

12:30.336 --> 12:32.066 align:middle
on the real economy
and on inflation,

12:32.736 --> 12:35.246 align:middle
taking into consideration
lags, as I mentioned.

12:35.466 --> 12:38.786 align:middle
We will be looking at
real rates, for example,

12:38.786 --> 12:42.136 align:middle
all across the yield curve and
all other financial conditions

12:42.136 --> 12:43.246 align:middle
as we make that assessment.

12:43.246 --> 12:43.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:44.716 --> 12:48.786 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

12:48.786 --> 12:50.846 align:middle
Hi, Howard Schneider
with Reuters.

12:50.906 --> 12:54.366 align:middle
I'm sure there's going to be
tons of confusion out there

12:54.366 --> 12:55.526 align:middle
about whether this
means you're going

12:55.526 --> 12:56.886 align:middle
to slow in December or not.

12:56.886 --> 12:59.676 align:middle
Would you say that the
bias right now is not

12:59.836 --> 13:01.946 align:middle
for another 75 basis
point increase?

13:02.586 --> 13:03.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:03.746 --> 13:08.466 align:middle
So, what I want to do is
put that question of pace

13:08.576 --> 13:12.146 align:middle
in the context of our broader
tightening program, if I may,

13:12.746 --> 13:15.516 align:middle
and talk about the statement
language along the way.

13:15.886 --> 13:18.286 align:middle
So I think you can think
about our tightening program

13:18.286 --> 13:20.396 align:middle
as really addressing
three questions,

13:20.526 --> 13:23.056 align:middle
the first of which was, and
has been, how fast to go.

13:23.736 --> 13:26.556 align:middle
The second is how high
to raise our policy rate.

13:26.816 --> 13:29.706 align:middle
And the third will be,
eventually, how long to remain

13:29.776 --> 13:30.996 align:middle
at a restrictive level.

13:31.086 --> 13:33.826 align:middle
So on the first question-how
fast

13:33.826 --> 13:36.646 align:middle
to tighten policy-it's
been very important

13:36.686 --> 13:39.316 align:middle
that we move expeditiously,
and we have clearly done so.

13:39.916 --> 13:42.796 align:middle
We've moved 3  percent
since March,

13:42.796 --> 13:44.556 align:middle
admittedly from a base of zero.

13:45.136 --> 13:46.696 align:middle
It's a historically fast pace,

13:46.696 --> 13:50.486 align:middle
and that's certainly appropriate
given the persistence

13:50.486 --> 13:52.176 align:middle
and strength in inflation
and the low level

13:52.176 --> 13:52.986 align:middle
from which we started.

13:53.696 --> 13:56.096 align:middle
So now we come to the second
question, which is how high

13:56.096 --> 13:57.206 align:middle
to raise our policy rate.

13:57.276 --> 13:59.606 align:middle
And we're saying that
we'd raise that rate

13:59.606 --> 14:02.306 align:middle
to a level that's sufficiently
restrictive to bring inflation

14:02.306 --> 14:04.476 align:middle
to our 2 percent
target over time,

14:04.706 --> 14:07.746 align:middle
and we put that into our
postmeeting statement

14:08.396 --> 14:11.146 align:middle
because that really does
become the important question,

14:11.146 --> 14:13.876 align:middle
we think now, is how far to go.

14:13.876 --> 14:15.716 align:middle
And I'll talk more about that.

14:16.296 --> 14:19.616 align:middle
We think there's some ground to
cover before we meet that test,

14:19.616 --> 14:20.366 align:middle
and that's why we say

14:20.366 --> 14:22.366 align:middle
that ongoing rate increases
will be appropriate.

14:22.886 --> 14:25.816 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, incoming
data between the meetings,

14:25.886 --> 14:27.316 align:middle
both a strong labor
market report

14:27.316 --> 14:30.816 align:middle
but particularly the CPI
report, do suggest to me

14:30.816 --> 14:34.366 align:middle
that we may ultimately move to
higher levels than we thought

14:34.406 --> 14:36.116 align:middle
at the time of the
September meeting.

14:36.426 --> 14:38.226 align:middle
That level is very
uncertain, though,

14:38.226 --> 14:43.086 align:middle
and I would say we're
going to find it over time.

14:43.906 --> 14:47.586 align:middle
Of course, with the lags between
policy and economic activity,

14:47.586 --> 14:48.686 align:middle
there's a lot of uncertainty,

14:49.226 --> 14:52.036 align:middle
so we note that in determining
the pace of future increases,

14:52.036 --> 14:53.876 align:middle
we'll take into account
the cumulative tightening

14:53.956 --> 14:56.696 align:middle
of monetary policy
as well as the lags

14:56.696 --> 14:59.156 align:middle
with which monetary policy
affects economic activity

14:59.156 --> 14:59.686 align:middle
and inflation.

15:00.286 --> 15:03.366 align:middle
So I would say, as we
come closer to that level,

15:03.496 --> 15:06.016 align:middle
move more into restrictive
territory, the question

15:06.116 --> 15:07.956 align:middle
of speed becomes less important

15:07.956 --> 15:09.206 align:middle
than the second and
third questions.

15:09.446 --> 15:11.876 align:middle
And that's why I've said at
the last two press conferences

15:12.376 --> 15:13.916 align:middle
that at some point, it
will become appropriate

15:13.916 --> 15:15.996 align:middle
to slow the pace of increases.

15:16.416 --> 15:19.436 align:middle
So that time is coming,
and it may come as soon

15:19.436 --> 15:21.406 align:middle
as the next meeting
or the one after that.

15:21.636 --> 15:22.866 align:middle
No decision has been made.

15:22.866 --> 15:26.496 align:middle
It is likely we'll have
a discussion about this

15:26.606 --> 15:28.176 align:middle
at the next meeting,
a discussion.

15:28.736 --> 15:32.716 align:middle
To be clear, let me say again
[that] the question of when

15:32.716 --> 15:34.536 align:middle
to moderate the pace

15:34.536 --> 15:38.556 align:middle
of increases is now much less
important than the question

15:38.646 --> 15:40.796 align:middle
of how high to raise
rates and how long

15:40.796 --> 15:42.966 align:middle
to keep monetary
policy restricted,

15:42.966 --> 15:44.726 align:middle
which really will be
our principal focus.

15:45.016 --> 15:45.286 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

15:45.286 --> 15:46.516 align:middle
If I could follow up on that,

15:46.616 --> 15:50.306 align:middle
to what degree was there an
importance or weight given

15:50.306 --> 15:53.426 align:middle
to a need to signal
this possibility now,

15:53.636 --> 15:56.326 align:middle
given all the concerns,
really around the globe,

15:56.326 --> 16:02.446 align:middle
about Fed policy sort of driving
ahead and everybody else dealing

16:02.446 --> 16:03.836 align:middle
with their own stress
as a result?

16:03.836 --> 16:04.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:04.826 --> 16:09.396 align:middle
Well, I think I'm pleased

16:09.396 --> 16:11.246 align:middle
that we have moved
as fast as we have.

16:11.396 --> 16:12.846 align:middle
I don't think we've
overtightened.

16:12.846 --> 16:14.776 align:middle
I think there's-very
difficult to make a case

16:14.826 --> 16:17.566 align:middle
that our current
level is too tight,

16:17.566 --> 16:19.456 align:middle
given that inflation
still runs well

16:19.456 --> 16:20.866 align:middle
above the federal funds rate.

16:21.606 --> 16:26.066 align:middle
So I think that at this meeting,
as in the last two meetings,

16:26.066 --> 16:28.116 align:middle
as I've mentioned, I've said

16:28.216 --> 16:30.906 align:middle
that we-that there would come
a point, and this was a meeting

16:30.906 --> 16:33.306 align:middle
at which we had a discussion
about what that might mean.

16:33.876 --> 16:36.026 align:middle
And we did discuss this,
and, as I mentioned,

16:36.026 --> 16:37.466 align:middle
we'll discuss it
again in December.

16:37.986 --> 16:40.496 align:middle
But there's no-I
don't have any sense

16:40.496 --> 16:42.276 align:middle
that we've overtightened
or moved too fast.

16:42.276 --> 16:44.676 align:middle
I think it's been good
and a successful program

16:44.766 --> 16:47.196 align:middle
that we've gotten
this far this fast.

16:47.646 --> 16:50.836 align:middle
Remember, though, that we
still think there's a need

16:50.836 --> 16:54.976 align:middle
for ongoing rate increases,
and we have some ground left

16:54.976 --> 16:57.416 align:middle
to cover here, and
cover it we will.

16:57.416 --> 16:59.946 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:04.846 --> 17:06.236 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

17:06.606 --> 17:08.006 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

17:08.566 --> 17:11.356 align:middle
Chair Powell, core
PCE inflation on a 3-

17:11.356 --> 17:13.156 align:middle
or 6-month annualized basis

17:13.156 --> 17:15.056 align:middle
and on a 12-month
basis has been running

17:15.056 --> 17:17.756 align:middle
in the high 4s, close
to 5 percent.

17:18.296 --> 17:21.116 align:middle
Is there any reason to think
you won't have to raise rates

17:21.496 --> 17:24.646 align:middle
at least above that
level to be confident

17:24.786 --> 17:26.666 align:middle
that you are imparting
enough restraint

17:27.056 --> 17:28.426 align:middle
to bring inflation down?

17:28.986 --> 17:30.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:30.686 --> 17:34.886 align:middle
So this is the question of,
does the policy rate need to get

17:34.886 --> 17:35.816 align:middle
above the inflation rate?

17:35.816 --> 17:37.886 align:middle
And I would say, there are
a range of views on it.

17:37.886 --> 17:40.056 align:middle
That's the classic
"Taylor principle" view.

17:40.516 --> 17:43.076 align:middle
But I would think you'd
look more at a forward,

17:43.306 --> 17:48.796 align:middle
a forward-looking measure of
inflation to look at that.

17:48.926 --> 17:54.896 align:middle
But I think the answer is, we'll
want to get the policy rate

17:54.966 --> 17:56.306 align:middle
to a level where it is,

17:56.306 --> 17:58.396 align:middle
where the real interest
rate is positive.

17:58.396 --> 17:59.326 align:middle
We will want to do that.

17:59.726 --> 18:01.926 align:middle
I do not think of
it as the single

18:01.926 --> 18:03.096 align:middle
and only touchstone, though.

18:03.096 --> 18:06.496 align:middle
I think you put some weight on
that; you also put some weight

18:06.496 --> 18:07.736 align:middle
on rates across the curve.

18:07.736 --> 18:08.996 align:middle
Very few people borrow

18:09.486 --> 18:12.546 align:middle
at the short end-at the
federal funds rate, for example.

18:12.546 --> 18:14.386 align:middle
So households and businesses,

18:14.556 --> 18:17.206 align:middle
if they're [borrowing]-[there
are] very meaningfully positive

18:17.206 --> 18:19.116 align:middle
interest rates all
across the curve for them,

18:19.616 --> 18:21.496 align:middle
credit spreads are larger,

18:21.496 --> 18:23.676 align:middle
so borrowing rates are
significantly higher,

18:24.216 --> 18:27.646 align:middle
and I think financial conditions
have tightened quite a bit.

18:27.926 --> 18:30.276 align:middle
So I would look at that
as an important feature.

18:30.276 --> 18:33.736 align:middle
I'd put some weight on it, but
I wouldn't say it's something

18:33.926 --> 18:36.016 align:middle
that is the single,
dominant thing to look at.

18:36.016 --> 18:36.466 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

18:36.606 --> 18:39.916 align:middle
If I could follow up: What
is your best assessment,

18:39.916 --> 18:43.166 align:middle
or the staff's best assessment
right now, of the current rate

18:43.166 --> 18:44.746 align:middle
of underlying inflation?

18:45.286 --> 18:46.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:46.046 --> 18:47.576 align:middle
I don't have a specific
number for you there.

18:47.576 --> 18:50.366 align:middle
There are many, many
models that look at that.

18:50.456 --> 18:52.306 align:middle
And, I mean, one
way to look at it is

18:52.306 --> 18:55.216 align:middle
that it's a pretty
stationary object and that

18:55.216 --> 18:59.886 align:middle
when inflation runs-above that
level, for sure-substantially

18:59.886 --> 19:01.606 align:middle
above for some time,
you'll see it move up,

19:01.606 --> 19:03.776 align:middle
but the movement will
be fairly gradual.

19:04.436 --> 19:08.296 align:middle
So I think that's what the
principal models would tend

19:08.296 --> 19:08.596 align:middle
to say.

19:08.596 --> 19:10.796 align:middle
But I wouldn't want to
land on any one assessment.

19:10.796 --> 19:12.686 align:middle
There are many different-as
you know,

19:12.776 --> 19:15.196 align:middle
many different people
publish an assessment

19:15.196 --> 19:16.126 align:middle
of underlying inflation.

19:16.436 --> 19:18.076 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

19:18.196 --> 19:18.496 align:middle
Thank you.

19:18.616 --> 19:19.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:19.066 --> 19:20.936 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

19:21.326 --> 19:21.866 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

19:21.906 --> 19:23.026 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

19:23.106 --> 19:25.076 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek with
the New York Times.

19:25.076 --> 19:27.356 align:middle
I wondered, do you see
any evidence at this stage

19:27.356 --> 19:30.876 align:middle
that inflation is, or is at
risk of becoming, entrenched?

19:31.666 --> 19:32.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:33.976 --> 19:35.476 align:middle
Is inflation becoming
entrenched?

19:35.646 --> 19:40.806 align:middle
So I guess I would start by
pointing to expectations.

19:40.906 --> 19:44.566 align:middle
So if we saw longer-term
expectations moving up,

19:44.616 --> 19:45.636 align:middle
that would be very troubling.

19:45.716 --> 19:48.366 align:middle
And they were moving up a
little bit at the middle part

19:48.366 --> 19:49.966 align:middle
of this year, and they've
moved now back down.

19:50.266 --> 19:51.686 align:middle
That's one piece of data.

19:52.486 --> 19:54.486 align:middle
Shorter-term inflation
expectations moved

19:54.486 --> 19:58.866 align:middle
up between the last
meeting and this meeting,

19:59.386 --> 20:01.486 align:middle
and we don't think
those are as indicative,

20:01.566 --> 20:04.016 align:middle
but they may be important
in the wage-setting process.

20:04.106 --> 20:05.616 align:middle
There's a school of
thought that believes that.

20:06.136 --> 20:07.336 align:middle
So that's very concerning.

20:07.946 --> 20:10.936 align:middle
I guess the other
thing I would say is

20:10.936 --> 20:14.446 align:middle
that the longer we have-we're
now 18 months into this episode

20:14.526 --> 20:20.486 align:middle
of high inflation, and we don't
have a clearly identified,

20:20.486 --> 20:22.476 align:middle
scientific way of understanding

20:22.926 --> 20:25.906 align:middle
at what point inflation
becomes entrenched.

20:26.526 --> 20:29.226 align:middle
And so the thing we need to do

20:29.226 --> 20:30.626 align:middle
from a risk-management
standpoint is

20:30.626 --> 20:35.026 align:middle
to use our tools forcefully but
thoughtfully and get inflation

20:35.026 --> 20:37.726 align:middle
under control-get it down to
2 percent-get it behind us.

20:38.086 --> 20:39.336 align:middle
That's what we really need to do

20:39.336 --> 20:41.096 align:middle
and what we're strongly
committed to doing.

20:42.646 --> 20:43.546 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:43.706 --> 20:47.376 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

20:47.766 --> 20:48.486 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

20:48.486 --> 20:49.936 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

20:49.936 --> 20:51.506 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

20:51.996 --> 20:54.036 align:middle
The statement points
to lag times.

20:54.186 --> 20:55.296 align:middle
I'm wondering if you can walk us

20:55.296 --> 20:57.616 align:middle
through how you judge
those lag effects,

20:57.616 --> 20:59.656 align:middle
what that timeline looks
like over the coming months

20:59.656 --> 21:02.096 align:middle
or even a year, and where
you would expect it to show

21:02.096 --> 21:03.316 align:middle
up in different parts
of the economy.

21:04.516 --> 21:05.086 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:05.386 --> 21:13.036 align:middle
So the way I would think
about that is, it's commonly,

21:13.216 --> 21:16.986 align:middle
for a long time, thought that
monetary policy works with "long

21:16.986 --> 21:20.106 align:middle
and variable lags,"
and that it works first

21:20.106 --> 21:22.836 align:middle
on financial conditions and
then on economic activity

21:22.836 --> 21:24.956 align:middle
and then perhaps later than
that even on inflation.

21:25.156 --> 21:27.186 align:middle
So that's been the
thinking for a long time.

21:27.776 --> 21:30.636 align:middle
There was an old literature
that made those lags

21:30.636 --> 21:32.906 align:middle
out to be fairly long.

21:32.956 --> 21:35.006 align:middle
There's newer literature that
says that they're shorter.

21:35.196 --> 21:37.576 align:middle
The truth is, we don't have
a lot of data [consisting]

21:37.926 --> 21:40.196 align:middle
of inflation of [rates]
this high in what is,

21:40.196 --> 21:41.116 align:middle
now, the modern economy.

21:41.506 --> 21:45.506 align:middle
One big difference now
is that it used to be

21:45.506 --> 21:47.426 align:middle
that you would raise
the federal funds rate,

21:47.776 --> 21:49.156 align:middle
financial conditions
would react,

21:49.806 --> 21:52.216 align:middle
and then that would affect
economic activity and inflation.

21:52.376 --> 21:57.136 align:middle
Now financial conditions react
well before in expectation

21:57.336 --> 21:58.306 align:middle
of monetary policy [actions].

21:58.506 --> 22:01.346 align:middle
That's the way it has
moved for a quarter

22:01.346 --> 22:03.756 align:middle
of a century-in the direction
of financial conditions,

22:03.836 --> 22:07.896 align:middle
then monetary policy-because
the markets are thinking,

22:08.136 --> 22:10.756 align:middle
what is the central
bank going to do?

22:10.756 --> 22:14.176 align:middle
And there are plenty of
economists that also think

22:14.176 --> 22:17.446 align:middle
that once financial conditions
change, that the effects

22:17.446 --> 22:19.316 align:middle
on the economy are
actually faster

22:19.316 --> 22:20.406 align:middle
than they would have
been before.

22:20.636 --> 22:21.656 align:middle
We don't know that.

22:21.656 --> 22:23.746 align:middle
I guess the thing
that I would say is:

22:23.746 --> 22:25.836 align:middle
It's highly uncertain-highly
uncertain.

22:25.836 --> 22:28.476 align:middle
And so from a risk-management
standpoint-but we do need,

22:28.476 --> 22:31.226 align:middle
it would be irresponsible
not to, to ignore them.

22:31.776 --> 22:34.566 align:middle
But you want to consider them
but not take them literally.

22:34.566 --> 22:37.626 align:middle
So I think it's a very difficult
place to be, but I would tend

22:37.626 --> 22:40.546 align:middle
to be-want to be in the
middle looking carefully

22:40.546 --> 22:44.726 align:middle
at what's actually happening
with the economy and trying

22:44.726 --> 22:47.016 align:middle
to make good decisions from
a risk-management standpoint,

22:47.516 --> 22:51.096 align:middle
remembering, of course, that
if we were to overtighten,

22:51.416 --> 22:54.846 align:middle
we could then use our tools
strongly to support the economy,

22:54.916 --> 22:59.586 align:middle
whereas if we don't get
inflation under control

22:59.646 --> 23:02.226 align:middle
because we don't tighten
enough, now we're in a situation

23:02.226 --> 23:04.576 align:middle
where inflation will become
entrenched and the costs,

23:05.096 --> 23:06.606 align:middle
the employment costs
in particular,

23:07.096 --> 23:08.816 align:middle
will be much higher potentially.

23:08.816 --> 23:12.166 align:middle
So from a risk-management
standpoint, we want to be sure

23:12.476 --> 23:15.796 align:middle
that we don't make the
mistake of either failing

23:15.796 --> 23:18.236 align:middle
to tighten enough or
loosening policy too soon.

23:18.236 --> 23:18.536 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

23:19.156 --> 23:21.536 align:middle
And if I could follow up:
Should we interpret the addition

23:21.536 --> 23:23.796 align:middle
to the statement to mean
that more weight is put

23:23.796 --> 23:25.716 align:middle
into those lag effects
than they would have been

23:25.976 --> 23:27.076 align:middle
after previous rate hikes?

23:27.146 --> 23:27.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:27.596 --> 23:33.936 align:middle
I think as we move now
into restrictive territory,

23:33.936 --> 23:35.726 align:middle
as we make these
ongoing rate hikes

23:35.726 --> 23:37.586 align:middle
and policy becomes
more restrictive,

23:38.016 --> 23:40.476 align:middle
it'll be appropriate now to
be thinking more about lags.

23:40.476 --> 23:43.406 align:middle
Of course, we think about
lags-the lags are just sort

23:43.406 --> 23:45.986 align:middle
of a basic part of
monetary policy.

23:45.986 --> 23:48.576 align:middle
But we will be thinking
about them,

23:49.056 --> 23:52.536 align:middle
but we won't be-I think
we'll be considering them

23:52.536 --> 23:54.076 align:middle
but because it's
appropriate to do so.

23:54.076 --> 23:58.656 align:middle
Let me say this: It
is very premature

23:58.786 --> 23:59.956 align:middle
to be thinking about pausing.

24:00.116 --> 24:02.746 align:middle
So people, when they hear
lags, they think about a pause.

24:02.746 --> 24:06.826 align:middle
It's very premature, in my view,
to think about or be talking

24:06.826 --> 24:08.676 align:middle
about pausing our rate hike.

24:08.746 --> 24:10.196 align:middle
We have a ways to go.

24:10.196 --> 24:15.666 align:middle
Our policy-we need ongoing
rate hikes to get to that level

24:15.766 --> 24:17.986 align:middle
of sufficiently restrictive.

24:17.986 --> 24:20.096 align:middle
And we don't-of course, we
don't really know exactly

24:20.096 --> 24:20.676 align:middle
where that is.

24:20.676 --> 24:23.566 align:middle
We have a sense, and we'll
write down in September-sorry,

24:23.566 --> 24:25.886 align:middle
in the December meeting,
a new Summary

24:25.886 --> 24:28.646 align:middle
of Economic Projections,
which updates that.

24:28.696 --> 24:31.176 align:middle
But I would expect us to
continue to update it based

24:31.176 --> 24:32.846 align:middle
on what we're seeing
with incoming data.

24:33.266 --> 24:33.556 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:34.376 --> 24:34.956 align:middle
Neil Irwin.

24:35.636 --> 24:37.096 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

24:37.456 --> 24:38.276 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

24:38.276 --> 24:39.516 align:middle
Neil Irwin, Axios.

24:40.086 --> 24:42.136 align:middle
As you look around the
economy, the clearest impact

24:42.136 --> 24:44.426 align:middle
of your tightening so
far has been on housing,

24:44.426 --> 24:46.366 align:middle
maybe some venture-funded
tech companies.

24:46.576 --> 24:48.616 align:middle
It's been relatively narrow
in terms of the labor market,

24:48.616 --> 24:50.166 align:middle
consumer demand;
a lot of sectors,

24:50.166 --> 24:51.966 align:middle
you don't see a ton of effect.

24:52.056 --> 24:54.166 align:middle
Is the pathway and channels

24:54.166 --> 24:55.866 align:middle
through which monetary
policy works changing?

24:55.866 --> 24:57.136 align:middle
Is it narrower than
it used to be?

24:57.466 --> 25:00.636 align:middle
And on housing in particular,
are you at all worried

25:00.636 --> 25:02.416 align:middle
that you're cramping
housing supply in ways

25:02.616 --> 25:04.076 align:middle
that might cause
problems down the road?

25:04.336 --> 25:04.786 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:04.786 --> 25:05.856 align:middle
I don't know that the channels

25:05.886 --> 25:07.956 align:middle
through which policy works
have changed that much.

25:07.956 --> 25:10.046 align:middle
I would say a big channel
is the labor market,

25:10.046 --> 25:11.796 align:middle
and the labor market
is very, very strong.

25:11.936 --> 25:12.606 align:middle
Very strong.

25:12.606 --> 25:17.226 align:middle
And households, of course,
have strong balance sheets.

25:17.226 --> 25:22.616 align:middle
So we go into this with a strong
labor market and excess demand

25:22.616 --> 25:24.086 align:middle
in the labor market,
as you can see

25:24.086 --> 25:27.126 align:middle
through many different things,
and also with households

25:27.126 --> 25:30.146 align:middle
who have strong spending
power built up.

25:30.676 --> 25:34.376 align:middle
So it may take time, it may take
resolve, it may take patience.

25:34.376 --> 25:36.336 align:middle
It's likely to, to
get inflation down.

25:36.506 --> 25:39.226 align:middle
It may-I think you see, from
our forecasts and others,

25:39.226 --> 25:41.956 align:middle
that it will take some time
for inflation to come down.

25:41.956 --> 25:43.406 align:middle
It'll take time, we think.

25:44.026 --> 25:46.996 align:middle
So, sorry, was I getting
to your question there?

25:47.666 --> 25:47.916 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

25:47.976 --> 25:48.456 align:middle
Housing. CHAIR POWELL.

25:48.526 --> 25:49.746 align:middle
Oh, housing-the housing
part of it.

25:49.796 --> 25:53.316 align:middle
Yes, so we look at
housing-of course,

25:53.316 --> 25:56.306 align:middle
housing is significantly
affected by these higher rates,

25:56.866 --> 25:59.496 align:middle
which are really back where they
were before the Global Financial

25:59.496 --> 26:00.966 align:middle
Crisis-they're not
historically high,

26:00.966 --> 26:02.426 align:middle
but they're much higher
than they've been.

26:03.006 --> 26:05.436 align:middle
And you're seeing
housing activity decline,

26:05.436 --> 26:09.216 align:middle
and you're seeing housing prices
growing at a faster rate and,

26:09.216 --> 26:11.296 align:middle
in some parts of the
country, declining.

26:11.906 --> 26:16.686 align:middle
You know, I would say housing
was-the housing market was very

26:16.686 --> 26:19.356 align:middle
overheated for the couple
of years after the pandemic

26:19.356 --> 26:21.386 align:middle
as demand increased
and rates were low.

26:21.386 --> 26:23.816 align:middle
We all know the stories

26:23.816 --> 26:27.016 align:middle
of how overheated the housing
market was, prices going up.

26:27.676 --> 26:30.906 align:middle
Many, many bidders and no
conditions, that kind of thing.

26:30.906 --> 26:33.726 align:middle
So the housing market needs
to get back into a balance

26:33.726 --> 26:34.886 align:middle
between supply and demand.

26:35.356 --> 26:38.406 align:middle
We're well aware of
what's going on there.

26:38.836 --> 26:41.886 align:middle
From a financial stability
standpoint, we didn't see

26:41.886 --> 26:45.766 align:middle
in this cycle the kinds of
poor credit underwriting

26:45.766 --> 26:47.666 align:middle
that we saw before the
Global Financial Crisis.

26:47.916 --> 26:51.746 align:middle
Housing credit was very
carefully-much more carefully

26:51.746 --> 26:53.006 align:middle
managed by the lenders.

26:53.446 --> 26:55.796 align:middle
So it's a very different
situation

26:55.796 --> 26:59.226 align:middle
and doesn't present potential
financial-doesn't appear

26:59.226 --> 27:00.946 align:middle
to present financial
stability issues.

27:01.816 --> 27:04.686 align:middle
But, no, we do understand
that that's really

27:04.686 --> 27:06.796 align:middle
where a very big
effect of our policy is.

27:08.596 --> 27:08.946 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:08.946 --> 27:11.376 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

27:11.916 --> 27:13.406 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

27:13.406 --> 27:16.536 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
the labor market.

27:16.536 --> 27:19.086 align:middle
You mentioned early on, again,

27:19.086 --> 27:22.546 align:middle
that job openings are very high
compared to available workers,

27:22.546 --> 27:26.016 align:middle
and I'm just curious
to what extent you do

27:26.016 --> 27:27.966 align:middle
and don't draw signal from that.

27:27.966 --> 27:32.056 align:middle
So, for example, if
wage growth is slowing

27:32.056 --> 27:34.496 align:middle
and if maybe the unemployment
rate starts to tick up,

27:34.866 --> 27:36.176 align:middle
will that make you sort

27:36.176 --> 27:38.936 align:middle
of decrease your
focus on job openings?

27:38.936 --> 27:41.746 align:middle
What do you see-are wages
what's really important?

27:41.746 --> 27:43.246 align:middle
How are you thinking
about the labor market

27:43.246 --> 27:44.256 align:middle
as it relates to inflation?

27:44.436 --> 27:44.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:44.946 --> 27:47.106 align:middle
So we talk a lot about vacancies

27:47.106 --> 27:49.696 align:middle
in the-vacancy-to-unemployed
rate, but it's just one,

27:49.696 --> 27:51.156 align:middle
it's just another data series.

27:51.496 --> 27:53.836 align:middle
It's been unusually
important in this cycle

27:53.836 --> 27:55.216 align:middle
because it's been
so out of line.

27:55.566 --> 27:56.636 align:middle
But so has quits.

27:56.916 --> 27:57.716 align:middle
So have wages.

27:57.716 --> 28:00.436 align:middle
So we look at a very
wide range of data

28:01.136 --> 28:03.556 align:middle
on the unemployment-on
the labor market.

28:03.556 --> 28:06.376 align:middle
So I'd start with unemployment,

28:06.376 --> 28:09.246 align:middle
which is typically the single
statistic you would look to,

28:09.246 --> 28:11.256 align:middle
is at a 50-year low,
3½ percent.

28:11.586 --> 28:14.586 align:middle
We're getting really
nothing in labor supply now.

28:14.636 --> 28:16.886 align:middle
We had, I think, very
small increase this year,

28:17.386 --> 28:19.626 align:middle
which we had really thought, we
thought we would get that back.

28:19.626 --> 28:22.716 align:middle
Most analysts thought we would
get some labor supply coming in.

28:23.346 --> 28:26.056 align:middle
You mentioned wages, so I
guess I would characterize

28:26.056 --> 28:27.716 align:middle
that as sort of a mixed picture.

28:27.716 --> 28:29.306 align:middle
It's true, with average
hourly earnings,

28:29.306 --> 28:31.286 align:middle
you see-so I would
call it a flattening

28:31.286 --> 28:33.926 align:middle
out at a level that's
well above the level

28:33.926 --> 28:36.886 align:middle
that would be consistent over
time with 2 percent inflation,

28:37.456 --> 28:41.166 align:middle
assuming a reasonable
productivity.

28:41.626 --> 28:44.156 align:middle
With the ECI reading this
week, again, a mixed picture.

28:44.156 --> 28:46.566 align:middle
The headline number
was a disappointment.

28:46.566 --> 28:47.916 align:middle
Let's just say it was high.

28:47.916 --> 28:48.926 align:middle
It didn't show a decline.

28:48.926 --> 28:53.196 align:middle
There's some rays of light
inside that if you look

28:53.196 --> 28:55.456 align:middle
at private-sector
workers-that did come down,

28:55.456 --> 28:56.756 align:middle
that compensation did come down.

28:57.086 --> 28:59.676 align:middle
Overall, though, the
broader picture is

28:59.746 --> 29:01.816 align:middle
of an overheated labor market

29:01.886 --> 29:04.436 align:middle
where demand substantially
exceeds supply.

29:05.146 --> 29:10.256 align:middle
Job creation still
exceeds sort of the level

29:10.256 --> 29:12.426 align:middle
that would hold the
market where it is.

29:12.906 --> 29:15.356 align:middle
So that's the picture.

29:15.416 --> 29:20.656 align:middle
Do we see-we keep looking for
signs that sort of the beginning

29:20.656 --> 29:23.036 align:middle
of a gradual softening
is happening.

29:23.036 --> 29:26.306 align:middle
And maybe that's there,
but it's not obvious to me,

29:26.306 --> 29:28.486 align:middle
because wages aren't coming
down-they're just moving

29:28.486 --> 29:30.656 align:middle
sideways at an elevated
level, both ECI

29:30.926 --> 29:32.126 align:middle
and average hourly earnings.

29:32.786 --> 29:35.656 align:middle
We want to see-we would love

29:35.656 --> 29:38.536 align:middle
to see vacancies coming
down, quits coming down.

29:38.596 --> 29:39.456 align:middle
They are coming down.

29:39.796 --> 29:43.746 align:middle
Vacancies are below their
all-time high but not by as much

29:43.746 --> 29:46.606 align:middle
as we thought, because-and
the data series is volatile,

29:46.606 --> 29:50.116 align:middle
we never take any one reading,
we always look at two or three.

29:50.546 --> 29:52.116 align:middle
So it's a mixed picture.

29:52.366 --> 29:56.756 align:middle
I don't see the case for
real softening just yet.

29:57.036 --> 29:59.856 align:middle
But we look at, I guess
as I just showed you,

29:59.856 --> 30:02.416 align:middle
we look at a very broad range
of data on the labor market.

30:02.606 --> 30:02.966 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

30:02.966 --> 30:06.176 align:middle
So do you see wages as being a
significant driver of inflation?

30:07.796 --> 30:08.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:08.156 --> 30:10.886 align:middle
I think wages have an
effect on inflation

30:10.886 --> 30:12.416 align:middle
and inflation has
an effect on wages.

30:12.416 --> 30:14.026 align:middle
I think that's always
been the case.

30:14.436 --> 30:17.526 align:middle
There's always-going back
and forth, the question is,

30:17.526 --> 30:19.826 align:middle
is that really elevated
right now?

30:19.826 --> 30:20.616 align:middle
I don't think so.

30:21.126 --> 30:23.756 align:middle
I don't think wages
are the principal story

30:23.796 --> 30:25.666 align:middle
of why prices are going up.

30:25.986 --> 30:26.736 align:middle
I don't think that.

30:27.156 --> 30:32.266 align:middle
I also don't think that we see
a wage-price spiral, but, again,

30:32.266 --> 30:37.146 align:middle
it's not something you can-once
you see it, you're in trouble.

30:37.146 --> 30:39.006 align:middle
So we don't want to see it.

30:39.006 --> 30:40.876 align:middle
We want wages to go up-we
just want them to go

30:40.876 --> 30:43.606 align:middle
up at a level that's
sustainable and consistent

30:43.606 --> 30:44.636 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation.

30:45.486 --> 30:49.576 align:middle
And we think we can-we do
think that, given the data

30:49.576 --> 30:53.996 align:middle
that we have, that this labor
market can soften without having

30:53.996 --> 30:56.086 align:middle
to soften as much as
history would indicate

30:56.426 --> 30:57.906 align:middle
through the unemployment
channel.

30:58.106 --> 31:00.806 align:middle
It can soften through
job openings declining.

31:01.206 --> 31:03.036 align:middle
We think there's room for that.

31:03.186 --> 31:05.046 align:middle
But we won't know
that-that'll be,

31:05.216 --> 31:06.546 align:middle
that'll be discovered
empirically.

31:08.196 --> 31:10.586 align:middle
KAYLA TAUSCHE.

31:12.396 --> 31:13.076 align:middle
Thank you so much.

31:13.076 --> 31:14.886 align:middle
Kayla Tausche from CNBC.

31:15.816 --> 31:18.956 align:middle
Earlier last month, the
United Nations warned

31:18.956 --> 31:20.626 align:middle
that there could be
a global recession

31:20.626 --> 31:22.576 align:middle
if central banks
didn't change course.

31:22.666 --> 31:24.876 align:middle
The new U.K. prime
minister warned

31:24.876 --> 31:27.626 align:middle
of a profound economic
crisis there.

31:27.916 --> 31:30.656 align:middle
And I'm wondering how the
Fed is weighing international

31:30.656 --> 31:34.926 align:middle
developments in light of a very
strong economy here in the U.S.

31:35.266 --> 31:36.866 align:middle
that would seem to be
bucking those trends.

31:37.596 --> 31:38.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:38.816 --> 31:44.626 align:middle
So, of course, we, we keep close
tabs on economic developments

31:44.626 --> 31:46.936 align:middle
and also geopolitical
developments that are relevant

31:47.206 --> 31:49.046 align:middle
to the economy abroad.

31:49.666 --> 31:51.806 align:middle
We're in very frequent contact

31:51.806 --> 31:58.216 align:middle
with our foreign counterparts
both through the IMF meetings

31:58.216 --> 32:00.816 align:middle
and the regular meetings with
central banks that we have,

32:00.816 --> 32:05.296 align:middle
and I have one this weekend
with many, many central bankers.

32:05.296 --> 32:07.576 align:middle
So we're in touch
with all of that.

32:08.336 --> 32:12.816 align:middle
So I guess what-it's clearly
a time, a difficult time

32:12.816 --> 32:16.356 align:middle
in the global economy.

32:16.506 --> 32:19.856 align:middle
We're seeing very high inflation
in Europe, significantly

32:19.856 --> 32:23.156 align:middle
because of high energy prices
related to the war in Ukraine.

32:23.976 --> 32:27.616 align:middle
And we're seeing
China's having issues

32:27.616 --> 32:31.826 align:middle
with the zero-COVID policy
and much slower growth

32:31.826 --> 32:32.796 align:middle
than we're used to seeing.

32:33.506 --> 32:35.796 align:middle
So we're seeing-we see
those difficulties.

32:35.796 --> 32:38.606 align:middle
The strong dollar is a
challenge for some countries.

32:38.606 --> 32:42.596 align:middle
But we haven't-we take all of
that into account in our models.

32:43.086 --> 32:45.126 align:middle
We think about the spillovers
and that sort of thing.

32:45.746 --> 32:48.246 align:middle
Here in the United States,
we have a strong economy,

32:49.006 --> 32:50.046 align:middle
and we have an economy

32:50.046 --> 32:51.936 align:middle
where inflation is
running at 5 percent.

32:52.016 --> 32:55.606 align:middle
Core PCE inflation-which
is a really good indicator

32:55.606 --> 32:59.616 align:middle
of what's going on for us,
the way we see it-is running

32:59.616 --> 33:02.926 align:middle
at 5.1 percent on a 12-month
basis and sort of similar

33:02.926 --> 33:04.946 align:middle
to that on a 3-, 6-,
and 9-month basis.

33:05.546 --> 33:07.686 align:middle
So we know that we
need to use our tools

33:07.726 --> 33:08.976 align:middle
to get inflation under control.

33:09.246 --> 33:10.666 align:middle
The world's not going
to be better off

33:10.666 --> 33:11.706 align:middle
if we fail to do that.

33:12.116 --> 33:13.686 align:middle
That's a task we need to do.

33:14.036 --> 33:17.896 align:middle
Price stability in the
United States is a good thing

33:17.896 --> 33:20.106 align:middle
for the global economy
over a long period of time.

33:20.316 --> 33:23.816 align:middle
Price stability is the kind
of thing that pays dividends

33:23.816 --> 33:26.156 align:middle
for our economy-for decades,

33:26.236 --> 33:29.226 align:middle
hopefully-even though it may
be difficult to get it back.

33:29.536 --> 33:33.166 align:middle
Getting it back is something
that gives-that provides value

33:33.166 --> 33:35.646 align:middle
to the people we serve
for the long run.

33:36.336 --> 33:36.696 align:middle
KAYLA TAUSCHE.

33:36.696 --> 33:41.606 align:middle
If I could just follow
up up on that-thank you.

33:41.776 --> 33:45.496 align:middle
The Fed has acknowledged
in the past that the tools

33:45.496 --> 33:48.126 align:middle
that you have don't
affect things like energy

33:48.126 --> 33:50.616 align:middle
and food prices that
stem from some

33:50.616 --> 33:52.686 align:middle
of those conflicts
overseas, and they're some

33:52.686 --> 33:54.476 align:middle
of the biggest pain
points for consumers.

33:54.476 --> 33:57.566 align:middle
So as you pursue the current
path that you've outlined,

33:57.896 --> 33:59.106 align:middle
is there a risk that some

33:59.106 --> 34:00.816 align:middle
of those prices simply
don't come down?

34:01.086 --> 34:02.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:02.296 --> 34:06.156 align:middle
So we don't directly
affect, for the most part,

34:06.156 --> 34:09.016 align:middle
food and energy prices, but the
demand channel does affect them

34:09.016 --> 34:09.906 align:middle
just at the margin.

34:10.566 --> 34:11.946 align:middle
The thing about the
United States is

34:11.976 --> 34:15.696 align:middle
that we also have strong-in
many other jurisdictions,

34:15.696 --> 34:17.966 align:middle
the principal problem
really is energy.

34:17.966 --> 34:20.166 align:middle
In the United States, we
also have a demand issue.

34:20.166 --> 34:22.726 align:middle
We've got an imbalance between
demand and supply, which you see

34:22.726 --> 34:24.146 align:middle
in many parts of the economy.

34:24.386 --> 34:27.946 align:middle
So our tools are well suited
to work on that problem,

34:27.946 --> 34:29.396 align:middle
and that's what we're doing.

34:29.396 --> 34:30.706 align:middle
You're right, though.

34:30.816 --> 34:33.986 align:middle
We don't-the price of
oil is set globally,

34:34.496 --> 34:36.446 align:middle
and it's not something
we can affect.

34:36.996 --> 34:39.116 align:middle
I think by the actions
that we take, though,

34:39.116 --> 34:43.776 align:middle
we help keep longer-term
inflation expectations anchored

34:44.306 --> 34:46.756 align:middle
and keep the public believing
in 2 percent inflation

34:46.756 --> 34:51.056 align:middle
by the things that we do, even
in times when energy is part

34:51.056 --> 34:52.696 align:middle
of the story of why
inflation is high.

34:53.016 --> 34:53.336 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:54.706 --> 34:58.396 align:middle
Jonnelle. JONNELLE MARTE.

34:58.396 --> 34:59.166 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

34:59.166 --> 35:00.476 align:middle
Jonnelle Marte with Bloomberg.

35:00.966 --> 35:06.026 align:middle
So the Fed is facing two
more ethics-related incidents

35:06.436 --> 35:09.066 align:middle
with the revision of
the financial statements

35:09.066 --> 35:10.306 align:middle
from President Bostic

35:10.386 --> 35:13.836 align:middle
and President Bullard
speaking at a closed event.

35:14.456 --> 35:16.956 align:middle
So some senators,
like Elizabeth Warren,

35:16.956 --> 35:18.656 align:middle
are saying that this is a sign

35:18.656 --> 35:22.526 align:middle
of greater ethics
problems at the Fed.

35:22.556 --> 35:26.366 align:middle
Could you talk about what this
does to the public's trust

35:26.366 --> 35:29.976 align:middle
in the bank and what the Fed
is doing to prevent this kind

35:29.976 --> 35:31.816 align:middle
of behavior from
becoming common?

35:32.936 --> 35:33.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:33.766 --> 35:37.676 align:middle
Sure. So you're right-the
public's trust is really the

35:37.676 --> 35:40.546 align:middle
Fed's and any central
bank's most important asset,

35:40.646 --> 35:44.526 align:middle
and any time one of us, one
of the policymakers, violates

35:44.526 --> 35:47.196 align:middle
or falls short of those
rules, we do risk undermining

35:47.196 --> 35:49.486 align:middle
that trust, and we take
that very seriously.

35:49.606 --> 35:54.246 align:middle
We do. So at the beginning of
our meeting yesterday, actually,

35:54.246 --> 35:56.406 align:middle
we had a Committee discussion
of the full Committee

35:56.946 --> 35:59.236 align:middle
on the importance of holding
ourselves individually

35:59.236 --> 36:01.036 align:middle
and collectively
accountable for knowing

36:01.036 --> 36:03.266 align:middle
and following the high
standard that's set

36:03.266 --> 36:05.516 align:middle
out in our existing
rules with respect

36:05.516 --> 36:07.286 align:middle
to both personal
investment activities

36:07.286 --> 36:08.716 align:middle
and external communications.

36:09.616 --> 36:12.096 align:middle
And we've taken a
number of steps,

36:12.196 --> 36:16.336 align:middle
and I would just say we do
understand how important those

36:16.336 --> 36:16.856 align:middle
issues are.

36:17.216 --> 36:22.736 align:middle
I would say that our
new investment program

36:22.736 --> 36:26.506 align:middle
that we have is up now and
running, and, actually,

36:26.506 --> 36:29.936 align:middle
it was through that
that the problems

36:30.016 --> 36:32.426 align:middle
with President Bostic's
disclosures were discovered,

36:32.426 --> 36:33.966 align:middle
when he filed his
new disclosure.

36:34.416 --> 36:37.786 align:middle
We now have a central group
here at the Board of Governors

36:38.256 --> 36:40.566 align:middle
that looks into disclosures
and follows them

36:40.566 --> 36:41.966 align:middle
and approves people's
disclosures

36:41.966 --> 36:43.376 align:middle
and also all of their trades.

36:43.376 --> 36:47.026 align:middle
Any trade anyone has to make
is covered, has to be approved,

36:47.086 --> 36:49.466 align:middle
preapproved, and
there's a lag-it has

36:49.466 --> 36:52.686 align:middle
to be preapproved 45
days before it happens,

36:52.686 --> 36:54.196 align:middle
so there's no ability
to game markets.

36:54.196 --> 36:55.666 align:middle
So it's a really good system.

36:55.806 --> 36:56.596 align:middle
It worked here.

36:57.336 --> 37:01.056 align:middle
And we-I think we all said to
each other today-yesterday,

37:01.056 --> 37:03.726 align:middle
actually, yesterday morning,
we recommitted to each other

37:03.726 --> 37:06.506 align:middle
and to this institution
to hold ourselves

37:06.506 --> 37:09.666 align:middle
to the highest standards
and avoid these problems.

37:10.446 --> 37:11.416 align:middle
JONNELLE MARTE.

37:11.416 --> 37:15.386 align:middle
Do you have an update on the
investigations that are pending?

37:15.386 --> 37:15.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:15.756 --> 37:17.716 align:middle
I don't. So, as you know,

37:18.046 --> 37:22.586 align:middle
I referred the matter
concerning President Bostic

37:22.656 --> 37:24.756 align:middle
to the inspector general,
and once that happens,

37:25.706 --> 37:28.936 align:middle
I don't discuss it with
the inspector general

37:28.936 --> 37:29.596 align:middle
or with anybody.

37:29.596 --> 37:31.436 align:middle
It's just-the inspector
general has,

37:31.626 --> 37:33.486 align:middle
he has the ability
to do investigations.

37:33.486 --> 37:34.676 align:middle
We don't really have that.

37:35.146 --> 37:36.046 align:middle
So that's what he's doing.

37:36.926 --> 37:37.876 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:37.876 --> 37:40.386 align:middle
Michael. MICHAEL MCKEE.

37:41.176 --> 37:43.546 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

37:44.166 --> 37:47.686 align:middle
Earlier this year, you touted
the three-month bill yield

37:47.686 --> 37:50.386 align:middle
out to 18 months
as the yield curve

37:50.536 --> 37:53.266 align:middle
with 100 percent
explanatory power.

37:53.356 --> 37:55.726 align:middle
And you said, quote,
"If it's inverted,

37:55.826 --> 37:57.496 align:middle
that means the Fed's
going to cut,

37:57.676 --> 37:59.416 align:middle
which means the economy
is weak."

37:59.986 --> 38:03.726 align:middle
That curve is only 2 basis
points away from inversion now,

38:03.726 --> 38:06.266 align:middle
so I'm wondering why
you are so confident

38:06.266 --> 38:10.106 align:middle
that you have not overtightened,
particularly given

38:10.276 --> 38:11.416 align:middle
that rates work with a lag.

38:11.896 --> 38:12.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:12.776 --> 38:15.956 align:middle
Well, so we do monitor
the near-term forward

38:15.956 --> 38:16.666 align:middle
spread-you're right.

38:16.666 --> 38:19.176 align:middle
And it's-that's been
our preferred measure.

38:19.176 --> 38:22.746 align:middle
We think, just empirically,
it dominates the ones

38:22.746 --> 38:24.436 align:middle
that people tend to
look at, which is 2s,

38:24.476 --> 38:25.506 align:middle
10s, and things like that.

38:25.506 --> 38:28.076 align:middle
So it's not inverted.

38:28.216 --> 38:29.686 align:middle
And, also, you have to look

38:29.686 --> 38:34.786 align:middle
at why things-why the rate
curve is doing what it's doing.

38:34.786 --> 38:37.356 align:middle
It can be doing that because
it affects-it expects cuts

38:37.356 --> 38:39.266 align:middle
or because it expects
inflation to come down.

38:39.676 --> 38:41.286 align:middle
In this case, if
you're in a situation

38:41.286 --> 38:44.346 align:middle
where the markets are pricing
in significant declines

38:44.346 --> 38:46.576 align:middle
in inflation, that's going
to affect the forward curve.

38:46.986 --> 38:48.286 align:middle
So, yes, we monitor it.

38:48.386 --> 38:48.946 align:middle
You're right.

38:48.946 --> 38:50.416 align:middle
And that's what I would say.

38:50.416 --> 38:52.596 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

38:53.216 --> 38:57.456 align:middle
If I could follow up: You
also said several meetings ago

38:57.456 --> 39:00.006 align:middle
that the risk of doing too
little outweighed the risk

39:00.006 --> 39:00.796 align:middle
of doing too much.

39:01.176 --> 39:02.896 align:middle
Is what you're trying
to tell us today is

39:02.896 --> 39:05.486 align:middle
that that risk assessment
has changed a little bit?

39:06.306 --> 39:06.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:07.166 --> 39:09.996 align:middle
Well, what's happened
is, time has passed,

39:09.996 --> 39:13.266 align:middle
and we've raised interest
rates by 375 basis points.

39:13.946 --> 39:16.736 align:middle
I would not-I would not change a
word in that statement, though.

39:16.926 --> 39:20.186 align:middle
I think until we
get inflation down,

39:20.426 --> 39:21.586 align:middle
you'll be hearing that from me.

39:21.826 --> 39:26.276 align:middle
Again, if we overtighten-and
we don't want to,

39:26.356 --> 39:29.416 align:middle
we want to get this exactly
right-but if we overtighten,

39:30.026 --> 39:32.956 align:middle
then we have the ability with
our tools, which are powerful,

39:32.956 --> 39:34.976 align:middle
to, as we showed
at the beginning

39:34.976 --> 39:37.266 align:middle
of the pandemic episode,

39:37.776 --> 39:39.656 align:middle
we can support economic
activity strongly

39:39.656 --> 39:41.396 align:middle
if that happens, if
that's necessary.

39:42.166 --> 39:44.216 align:middle
On the other hand, if
you make the mistake

39:44.216 --> 39:47.566 align:middle
in the other direction
and you let this drag on,

39:47.566 --> 39:50.306 align:middle
then it's a year or two down
the road, and you're realizing,

39:50.616 --> 39:52.546 align:middle
inflation behaving
the way it can,

39:52.616 --> 39:54.226 align:middle
you're realizing you
didn't actually get it,

39:54.226 --> 39:55.206 align:middle
you have to go back in.

39:55.786 --> 39:57.656 align:middle
By then, the risk, really,

39:57.656 --> 39:59.986 align:middle
is that it has become
entrenched in people's thinking.

40:00.456 --> 40:04.016 align:middle
And the record is that the
employment costs-the cost

40:04.016 --> 40:07.866 align:middle
to the people that we
don't want to hurt-they go

40:07.866 --> 40:09.956 align:middle
up with the passage of time.

40:10.206 --> 40:12.016 align:middle
That's really how I look at it.

40:12.016 --> 40:14.886 align:middle
So that isn't going to change.

40:14.886 --> 40:16.256 align:middle
What has changed,
though-you're right-is,

40:16.256 --> 40:17.476 align:middle
we're farther along now.

40:17.566 --> 40:19.366 align:middle
And I think as we're
farther along,

40:19.476 --> 40:21.766 align:middle
we're now focused on that.

40:21.826 --> 40:25.816 align:middle
What's the place, what's the
level we need to get to rates?

40:25.916 --> 40:28.306 align:middle
And I don't know what we'll do
when we get there, by the way.

40:28.306 --> 40:30.896 align:middle
It doesn't-we'll have to see.

40:30.896 --> 40:32.516 align:middle
There's been no decision
or discussion

40:32.516 --> 40:35.226 align:middle
around exactly what steps
we would take at that point.

40:35.286 --> 40:38.186 align:middle
But the first thing is
to find your way there.

40:39.876 --> 40:40.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:40.146 --> 40:41.266 align:middle
Chris Rugaber.

40:42.506 --> 40:43.006 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

40:43.006 --> 40:43.396 align:middle
Thank you.

40:43.586 --> 40:45.316 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

40:45.406 --> 40:48.596 align:middle
Just to go back to housing for a
minute: You mentioned the impact

40:48.596 --> 40:51.546 align:middle
that rate increases have had
on housing-home sales are

40:51.546 --> 40:54.936 align:middle
down 25 percent in the past
year, and so forth-but none

40:54.936 --> 40:57.046 align:middle
of this is really showing
up in, as you know,

40:57.046 --> 40:58.676 align:middle
in the government's
inflation measures.

40:59.226 --> 41:04.116 align:middle
And as we go forward, private
real-time data is clearly

41:04.116 --> 41:05.496 align:middle
showing these hits to housing.

41:05.586 --> 41:08.856 align:middle
Are you going to need to put a
greater weight on that in order

41:08.856 --> 41:13.626 align:middle
to ascertain things like whether
there's overtightening going on,

41:13.836 --> 41:15.596 align:middle
or will you still focus as much

41:15.596 --> 41:17.576 align:middle
on the more lagging
government indicators?

41:18.546 --> 41:18.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:18.836 --> 41:20.686 align:middle
So this is an interesting
subject.

41:20.686 --> 41:24.596 align:middle
So I start by saying, I
guess, that the measure that's

41:24.596 --> 41:28.936 align:middle
in the CPI and the
PCE, it captures rents

41:28.936 --> 41:31.766 align:middle
for all tenants,
not just new leases.

41:32.426 --> 41:34.246 align:middle
And that makes sense, actually,

41:34.246 --> 41:36.456 align:middle
because for that
reason-conceptually,

41:36.456 --> 41:41.046 align:middle
that is-that's sort of the right
target for monetary policy.

41:41.716 --> 41:44.976 align:middle
And the same thing is true
for owners' equivalent rent,

41:44.976 --> 41:48.846 align:middle
which comes off of-it's a
reweighting of tenant rents.

41:49.706 --> 41:54.406 align:middle
The private measures are, of
course, good at picking up,

41:54.406 --> 41:56.796 align:middle
at the margin, the new leases,

41:57.056 --> 41:59.616 align:middle
and they tell you
a couple things.

41:59.616 --> 42:04.406 align:middle
One thing is, I think right
now, if you look at the pattern

42:04.406 --> 42:08.506 align:middle
of that series of the new
leases, it's very pro-cyclical,

42:08.506 --> 42:12.476 align:middle
so rents went up much more
than the CPI and PCE rents did.

42:12.926 --> 42:14.396 align:middle
And now they're coming
down faster.

42:15.026 --> 42:18.896 align:middle
But what you're-the implication
is that there are still,

42:19.116 --> 42:25.956 align:middle
as people, as non-new leases
roll over and expire, right,

42:26.696 --> 42:28.806 align:middle
they're still in the pipeline,

42:28.806 --> 42:31.956 align:middle
there's still some significant
rate increases coming, okay?

42:32.226 --> 42:34.886 align:middle
But at some point, once
you get through that,

42:34.996 --> 42:37.916 align:middle
the new leases are going to tell
you-what they're telling you is,

42:37.916 --> 42:42.856 align:middle
there will come a point at
which rent inflation will start

42:42.856 --> 42:43.316 align:middle
to come down.

42:43.316 --> 42:45.706 align:middle
But that point is well
out from where we are now.

42:45.706 --> 42:50.486 align:middle
So we're well aware of that,
of course, and we look at it.

42:50.486 --> 42:53.076 align:middle
But I would say that in terms
of-the right way to think

42:53.076 --> 42:56.856 align:middle
about inflation, really,
is to look at the measure

42:56.856 --> 43:00.526 align:middle
that we do look at, but
considering that we also know

43:00.786 --> 43:03.326 align:middle
that at some point, you'll
see rents coming down.

43:03.826 --> 43:04.696 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

43:04.966 --> 43:06.446 align:middle
Great, and just a quick follow.

43:06.766 --> 43:09.876 align:middle
It looks like stock and bond
markets are reacting positively

43:09.876 --> 43:11.156 align:middle
to your announcement so far.

43:11.156 --> 43:13.936 align:middle
Is that something
you wanted to see?

43:13.936 --> 43:17.816 align:middle
Is that a problem or what-how

43:17.926 --> 43:20.186 align:middle
that might affect
your future policy

43:20.186 --> 43:21.936 align:middle
to see this positive reaction?

43:22.686 --> 43:23.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:23.996 --> 43:27.096 align:middle
We're not targeting any one
or two particular things.

43:27.096 --> 43:29.866 align:middle
Our message should be-what
I'm trying to do is make sure

43:29.866 --> 43:33.046 align:middle
that our message is clear, which
is that we think we have a ways

43:33.046 --> 43:34.436 align:middle
to go, we have some
ground to cover

43:34.436 --> 43:38.666 align:middle
with interest rates before
we get to, before we get

43:38.666 --> 43:41.526 align:middle
to that level of interest rates

43:41.526 --> 43:43.026 align:middle
that we think is
sufficiently restrictive.

43:43.266 --> 43:46.066 align:middle
And putting that in the
statement and identifying

43:46.066 --> 43:48.116 align:middle
that as a goal is
an important step.

43:48.686 --> 43:51.566 align:middle
And that's meant to put
that question, really,

43:51.566 --> 43:53.316 align:middle
as the important one
now, going forward.

43:54.056 --> 43:56.946 align:middle
I've also said that we think
that the level of rates

43:57.006 --> 43:58.226 align:middle
that we estimated

43:58.226 --> 44:00.566 align:middle
in September-the
incoming data suggests

44:00.566 --> 44:01.866 align:middle
that that's actually
going to be higher,

44:02.296 --> 44:03.216 align:middle
and that's been the pattern.

44:03.436 --> 44:05.126 align:middle
I mean, I would have
little confidence

44:05.126 --> 44:07.776 align:middle
that the forecast-if we
made a forecast today,

44:07.776 --> 44:10.736 align:middle
if we were doing an SEP today,
the pattern has been that,

44:11.006 --> 44:14.046 align:middle
one after another, they go up,
and that'll end when it ends,

44:14.046 --> 44:20.426 align:middle
but there's no sense that
inflation is coming down.

44:20.426 --> 44:24.816 align:middle
If you look at the-I have a
table of the last 12 months

44:24.816 --> 44:27.506 align:middle
of 12-month readings, and
there's really no pattern there.

44:27.506 --> 44:31.776 align:middle
We're exactly where
we were a year ago.

44:31.836 --> 44:36.726 align:middle
So I would also say, it's
premature to discuss pausing.

44:37.556 --> 44:40.366 align:middle
And it's not something
that we're thinking about.

44:40.616 --> 44:42.516 align:middle
That's really not a
conversation to be had now.

44:42.516 --> 44:43.676 align:middle
We have a ways to go.

44:43.676 --> 44:48.346 align:middle
And the last thing I'll say
is that I would want people

44:48.346 --> 44:50.536 align:middle
to understand our commitment
to getting this done

44:51.116 --> 44:55.996 align:middle
and to not making the mistake of
not doing enough or the mistake

44:55.996 --> 45:03.336 align:middle
of withdrawing our strong
policy and doing that too soon.

45:03.486 --> 45:07.606 align:middle
So those-I control those
messages, and that's my job.

45:08.886 --> 45:09.826 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:10.146 --> 45:11.856 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

45:11.856 --> 45:13.636 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

45:13.636 --> 45:14.636 align:middle
Thank you, Fed Chairman.

45:14.636 --> 45:18.326 align:middle
So how big of a headwind
is all the fiscal spending

45:18.566 --> 45:19.896 align:middle
to what the Federal
Reserve's trying to do

45:19.896 --> 45:21.676 align:middle
to get inflation back
to the 2 percent target?

45:23.016 --> 45:24.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:24.816 --> 45:27.646 align:middle
In theory, it was a
headwind this year,

45:27.976 --> 45:30.846 align:middle
but I do think the
broader context is

45:30.846 --> 45:34.656 align:middle
that you have households that
have these significant amounts

45:34.656 --> 45:40.176 align:middle
of savings and can keep spending
even in-so I think those two

45:40.176 --> 45:41.906 align:middle
things do tend to wage-to sort

45:41.906 --> 45:43.136 align:middle
of counterbalance
each other out.

45:43.396 --> 45:46.916 align:middle
It appears, consumer spending
is still positive-it's

45:46.916 --> 45:48.496 align:middle
at pretty modest growth levels.

45:48.956 --> 45:49.896 align:middle
It's not shrinking.

45:49.896 --> 45:54.646 align:middle
The banks that deal
with retail customers

45:55.176 --> 45:57.106 align:middle
and many retailers will tell you

45:57.106 --> 45:59.926 align:middle
that the consumers
are still buying

46:00.076 --> 46:02.126 align:middle
and they're still-they're fine.

46:02.126 --> 46:05.216 align:middle
So I don't know how big
the fiscal headwinds are,

46:05.216 --> 46:07.556 align:middle
and they haven't shown up in the
way that we thought they would

46:07.676 --> 46:08.746 align:middle
in restraining spending.

46:08.996 --> 46:11.916 align:middle
So it must have to do with
the savings that people have.

46:12.356 --> 46:12.696 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

46:12.696 --> 46:13.496 align:middle
So, what about the spending?

46:13.496 --> 46:15.636 align:middle
There's tens of billions
yet to be spent, I mean,

46:15.636 --> 46:17.656 align:middle
from the Inflation Reduction
Act, the American Rescue Plan,

46:17.916 --> 46:19.846 align:middle
CHIPS Act, bipartisan
infrastructure bill.

46:20.106 --> 46:23.136 align:middle
How does that play into your
thinking about the future?

46:23.826 --> 46:24.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:25.086 --> 46:27.766 align:middle
Demand is going to have some
support from those savings

46:27.866 --> 46:30.286 align:middle
and also from the strong
demand that's still

46:30.286 --> 46:30.896 align:middle
in the labor market.

46:30.896 --> 46:33.666 align:middle
We still see pretty
significant demand

46:33.666 --> 46:36.546 align:middle
and a tightening labor market in
some respects, although I think,

46:36.736 --> 46:40.126 align:middle
overall, I would say it's not
really tightening or loosening.

46:40.616 --> 46:42.936 align:middle
So we see those things, and
what those things tell us is

46:42.936 --> 46:46.836 align:middle
that our job is going
to require some resolve

46:46.836 --> 46:48.286 align:middle
and some patience over time.

46:48.386 --> 46:50.116 align:middle
We're going to have
to stick with this.

46:50.236 --> 46:54.416 align:middle
And that's just-we take
all that as a given,

46:54.946 --> 46:56.056 align:middle
but we know what
our objective is,

46:56.056 --> 46:57.456 align:middle
and we know what
our tools can do,

46:57.816 --> 47:00.346 align:middle
and that's how we
think about it.

47:00.346 --> 47:01.896 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:01.896 --> 47:04.056 align:middle
Thanks. We'll go to Nancy
for the last question.

47:04.196 --> 47:05.806 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

47:05.896 --> 47:06.536 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

47:06.536 --> 47:08.506 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
from Marketplace.

47:08.876 --> 47:13.796 align:middle
I'm wondering, has the window
for a soft landing narrowed?

47:14.156 --> 47:15.466 align:middle
Do you still think
it's possible?

47:15.606 --> 47:15.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:15.816 --> 47:17.556 align:middle
Has it narrowed?

47:17.556 --> 47:19.886 align:middle
Yes. Is it still possible?

47:19.966 --> 47:23.956 align:middle
Yes. We've always said it
was going to be difficult,

47:24.016 --> 47:26.946 align:middle
but I think to the extent
rates have to go higher

47:26.946 --> 47:29.306 align:middle
and stay higher for
longer, it becomes harder

47:29.306 --> 47:32.536 align:middle
to see the path-it's narrowed.

47:32.626 --> 47:34.316 align:middle
I would say the path has
narrowed over the course

47:34.316 --> 47:35.416 align:middle
of the last year, really.

47:36.016 --> 47:37.746 align:middle
Hard to say.

47:37.746 --> 47:39.206 align:middle
Hard to say.

47:39.316 --> 47:44.726 align:middle
Again, I would say that
the sort of array of data

47:44.726 --> 47:49.436 align:middle
in the labor market is highly
unusual and, to many economists,

47:49.526 --> 47:53.976 align:middle
there is a path to-ordinarily
there's a relationship

47:53.976 --> 47:59.696 align:middle
to GDP going down and
vacancies declining,

48:00.306 --> 48:03.676 align:middle
translating into unemployment,
or there's Okun's law.

48:03.786 --> 48:06.416 align:middle
So all those things are
relationships that are

48:06.416 --> 48:07.746 align:middle
in the data, and
they're very real.

48:07.746 --> 48:09.866 align:middle
Data's a little bit
different this time, though,

48:09.866 --> 48:11.756 align:middle
because you have this
tremendously high level

48:11.756 --> 48:14.756 align:middle
of vacancies and, we
think, on a very steep part

48:14.756 --> 48:15.486 align:middle
of the Beveridge curve.

48:16.316 --> 48:21.556 align:middle
All I would say is that the job
losses may turn out to be less

48:21.616 --> 48:25.026 align:middle
than would be indicated by
those traditional measures,

48:25.086 --> 48:28.506 align:middle
because job openings
are so elevated

48:28.506 --> 48:30.916 align:middle
and because the labor
market is so strong.

48:31.226 --> 48:32.026 align:middle
Again, that's going

48:32.026 --> 48:34.136 align:middle
to be something we
discover empirically.

48:34.266 --> 48:37.796 align:middle
I think no one knows whether
there's going to be a recession

48:37.796 --> 48:41.656 align:middle
or not and, if so, how bad
that recession would be.

48:42.476 --> 48:46.506 align:middle
And our job is to
restore price stability

48:46.506 --> 48:49.176 align:middle
so that we can have
a strong labor market

48:49.176 --> 48:50.456 align:middle
that benefits all over time.

48:50.536 --> 48:51.306 align:middle
And that's what we're
going to do.

48:51.306 --> 48:51.976 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

48:51.976 --> 48:53.316 align:middle
Just real quickly-why
do you feel

48:53.316 --> 48:54.346 align:middle
like the window has narrowed?

48:54.466 --> 48:56.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:56.746 --> 48:59.666 align:middle
Because we haven't seen
inflation coming down.

49:00.006 --> 49:02.936 align:middle
The implication of
inflation not coming down.

49:03.176 --> 49:07.366 align:middle
What we would expect by now to
have seen is that as the-really,

49:07.366 --> 49:10.996 align:middle
as the supply-side problems
have resolved themselves,

49:11.676 --> 49:14.476 align:middle
we would have expected
goods inflation to come

49:14.476 --> 49:16.516 align:middle
down by now, long since by now.

49:17.106 --> 49:17.876 align:middle
And it really hasn't.

49:17.876 --> 49:20.286 align:middle
Actually, it has come down,

49:20.286 --> 49:22.176 align:middle
but not to the extent
that we had hoped.

49:22.556 --> 49:24.936 align:middle
At the same time, now you
see services inflation,

49:24.936 --> 49:29.506 align:middle
core services inflation
moving up, and I just think

49:29.506 --> 49:33.056 align:middle
that the inflation picture has
become more and more challenging

49:33.056 --> 49:34.856 align:middle
over the course of this
year, without question.

49:35.366 --> 49:39.096 align:middle
That means that we have to have
policy be more restrictive,

49:39.546 --> 49:43.246 align:middle
and that narrows the path to
a soft landing, I would say.

49:57.936 --> 50:05.426 align:middle
Thanks very much.

